ncertSciG6ExploringMagnetsTeacherStudy = TeacherStudyData(
  chapterSnapshot: 'NCERT Class 6 Science (Curiosity) Chapter 4: Exploring Magnets (QR: 0677CH04). An experiential, physically grounded investigation into magnetic phenomena, field interactions, historical navigation, and technological applications. The chapter opens with Reshma\'s evocative story of ancient Kerala spice traders caught in a starless monsoon storm, introducing the revolutionary invention of the magnetic compass. It categorizes natural magnets (lodestones / magnetite) and manufactured artificial magnets into diverse geometric forms: bar, U-shaped (horseshoe), ring, disc, cylindrical, and spherical. It strictly classifies materials into magnetic (iron, steel, nickel, cobalt) and non-magnetic (wood, rubber, plastic, glass, copper, aluminium). Through hands-on experiments with iron filings, the chapter demonstrates that magnetic attraction is concentrated at two opposite extremities called the North and South poles, and proves that magnetic poles always occur in inseparable pairs (monopoles do not exist; fragmenting a magnet creates smaller complete dipolar magnets). It explains the fundamental directional property of freely suspended magnets aligning along the geographic North-South axis because Earth itself acts like a gigantic magnet. It celebrates India\'s maritime heritage with the "Matsya-yantra" (machchh-yantra)-a magnetized iron fish floating in oil used for oceanic navigation. It outlines the single-touch stroke method to magnetize iron needles and construct DIY floating compasses. It establishes the universal laws of magnetic interaction (like poles repel, unlike attract) and rigorously proves why Repulsion is the only definitive test of magnetism. It showcases non-contact magnetic penetration through non-magnetic media (wood, glass, cardboard, water), teaches best practices for preventing demagnetisation using soft iron magnetic keepers and wooden spacers, and introduces modern innovations like Maglev trains.',
