  completeExplanation: 'Class 6 Science Chapter 4 takes students on a physical and historical exploration of magnetism. The chapter starts with the narrative of Kerala spice traders who navigated overcast seas using magnetic direction-finding instruments. Students explore natural magnets-lodestones (magnetite rocks)-and learn how modern technology creates artificial permanent magnets in shapes suited to diverse engineering needs: bar, horseshoe (U-shaped), ring, disc, cylindrical, and spherical. Students learn through experimentation to classify everyday materials into magnetic substances (strongly attracted to magnets, such as iron, nickel, cobalt, and steel) and non-magnetic substances (unaffected by magnets, such as wood, plastic, rubber, paper, glass, and non-ferrous metals like copper and aluminium). By sprinkling iron filings over a bar magnet, students observe that magnetic attraction is concentrated at the two opposite ends, defined as the North pole and the South pole, while the central region exerts minimal force. The chapter emphasizes that magnetic monopoles are impossible: snapping a bar magnet in half creates two complete magnets, each with its own North and South poles. When a magnet is freely suspended by a thread, it rotates until it settles along the geographic North-South direction, revealing that Earth itself functions as a colossal magnet. This property underpins the magnetic compass, which features a lightweight magnetized needle (with its North-seeking end marked in red) pivoted over a directional card. The chapter highlights Indian nautical science by detailing the ancient "Matsya-yantra" (a fish-shaped iron magnet floating in oil) used centuries ago for oceanic navigation across the Indian Ocean. Students learn to magnetize an unmagnetized iron sewing needle using the single-touch method by stroking it in one direction 30 to 40 times with a bar magnet pole. Exploring magnetic interactions reveals that like poles repel (N-N and S-S) while unlike poles attract (N-S). Crucially, repulsion is shown to be the only definitive test of magnetism because an unmagnetized iron bar is attracted to both poles of a magnet, but only two like magnetic poles experience repulsion. Finally, the chapter demonstrates that magnetic force is a non-contact force that penetrates non-magnetic barriers like glass, water, and wood, and outlines methods to preserve magnet strength using soft iron magnetic keepers and wooden spacers to avoid demagnetisation from heat, shock, or impacts.',
